WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.820
The moral of The Prodigy is as classic as
horror movies go - it would be this: Creepy

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:06.440
kids sure are creepy!

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:10.430
But lurking under the surface of this tale
of alarming and unsettling offspring is a

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:13.840
deeper story of a mother's love and a child's
role in the world.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.840
So maybe you stuck with The Prodigy to its
sadistic end, or maybe you were too shocked

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.870
by the sight of all that stomach blood to
follow the final twists.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:24.029
Well, get ready, because we've got some major
spoilers ahead.

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:27.360
Let's dig back through the ending of The Prodigy
to figure out what it all really meant.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.800
Even though the movie takes awhile to build
up to the full reveal, the main premise of

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.860
The Prodigy is laid out right in the beginning.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:38.579
A series of spliced scenes reveal a police
task force raiding the compound of a mysterious

00:00:38.579 --> 00:00:42.899
serial killer at the exact same time a woman
named Sarah, played by Taylor Schilling, gives

00:00:42.899 --> 00:00:45.100
birth to a baby boy in a hospital.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.720
As soon as the man is shot to death, the infant
pops out.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:53.460
"Sedate her."

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.440
This scene introduces audiences to the building
blocks that this movie is constructed on.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.489
But more than that, it also lubricates the
audience's minds to the bizarre concepts of

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:06.720
reincarnation and soul transfer that come
into play towards the movie's end.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.960
By giving away the big reveal before the opening
credits even finish, The Prodigy has plenty

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.140
of room to play with those ideas without putting
the brakes on the story to explain how all

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:15.640
this happened.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.960
We already know: There's a serial killer's
soul living in Miles.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:20.220
He's gonna do bad things.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.760
Y'know, just like Chucky, but with a real
boy instead!

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.480
"I hate kids."

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:31.731
The Prodigy leans heavily on this important
idea of reincarnation — when you die, you

00:01:31.731 --> 00:01:33.420
come back to life in another body.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.299
But in this movie world, the process is sort
of like a ghost haunting an old house.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:41.480
That mean ol' spirit is only sticking around
because it left behind some unfinished business.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.140
"What in the hell is going on out here?

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.980
Get back in your graves."

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:53.140
But The Prodigy's plot points draw from more
than mystical gurus and religious beliefs.

00:01:53.149 --> 00:01:58.250
The DVD that reincarnation maestro Arthur
Jacobson gives to Sarah in the film also comes

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:03.000
from a real news investigation into a boy
who claimed to be a reincarnated World War

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:03.580
II pilot.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.820
"I can beat the Japanese easy as pie."

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:12.160
Clearly, the idea was to present all these
far-fetched themes as offshoots the real world,

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:16.410
anchoring Miles' predicament — and his eventual
outcome — within the realm of real-life

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:17.240
possibility.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.400
Eh, anyway...

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:23.020
So let's talk about Miles, the titular prodigy
and solid proof that kids should never do

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:24.200
their own Halloween makeup.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:29.000
This is a boy with two souls inside him, both
acting on his psyche at any given time.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.750
It turns out that he's not actually a prodigy
after all.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:36.390
All those special smarts are courtesy of Edward
Scarka, the crazy man who died at the beginning

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:37.000
of the movie.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.940
The movie plays with this duality constantly.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:44.220
From the heterochromia that gives Miles two
different colored eyes, to scenes with Miles'

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:48.310
face halfway obscured by shadow, we're given
no opportunity to forget that there's a killer

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:48.800
in there.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.960
The most iconic moment comes when Miles is
eight years old and wipes off one half of

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:54.400
his skeleton Halloween makeup.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:03:00.020
Staring into the mirror, he sees half of a
normal boy's face and half of a twisted skeleton's...with

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:01.720
an unhealthy interest in ladies' hands.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:06.190
It's around this time that Scarka's mind starts
entirely consuming the boy, leading him to

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:08.260
commit some pretty vicious acts.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.940
"Miles, what did you do?"

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:18.560
As The Prodigy enters its final act, it does
eventually stick a concrete direction..

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.920
Once Sarah accepts the fact that Miles is
possessed by another dude's soul, her working

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.640
theory is that Scarka has some unfinished
business that led him to stick around the

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.560
land of the living after his own death.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:33.340
And once she finds Miles-Scarka's stash of
reading material in Miles' bedroom, she knows

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:37.970
what that business is: Scarka needs to kill
Margaret St. James, played by Brittany Allen.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:41.470
You may remember her as the young woman who
got away from him and led the police to his

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:43.420
house in the opening scenes of the film.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:47.940
Sarah decides to help Scarka finish the job,
reasoning that doing so will free his soul

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.480
and cause him to leave Miles once and for
all.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.630
So Sarah buys a gun and takes her possessed
son on a road trip to the far-away land of

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:57.860
Ohio, intent on confronting St. James at her
house.

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:02.170
With this decision, The Prodigy veers out
of Bad Seed territory and takes us down a

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:02.900
new path.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:07.160
This isn't a family trying to survive their
evil progeny; this is a mother who will stop

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.750
at nothing to protect her son, even if it
means shooting an innocent woman.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:11.750
Second thoughts

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:16.200
The internal conflict Sarah faces as she contemplates
shooting Margaret St. James is compounded

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.790
by a shocking revelation — Margaret herself
also has children.

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:23.920
By shooting her, Sarah would only save her
own son at the expense of another mother.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:28.190
Like Miles' Halloween paint, the movie introduces
this mirrored circumstance with the help of

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:29.280
a literal mirror.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:33.840
Sarah loads her gun in Margaret's bathroom,
only to come face to face with her reflection.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.390
She realizes that, in a way, she'll be destroying
the life of someone very much like herself

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:40.760
in an effort to prevent the destruction of
her own.

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.220
In the end, Sarah can't do it.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:44.900
She drops the gun on the kitchen table and
accepts defeat.

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:49.380
That is, until Miles walks into the kitchen
with a big knife and plunges it into Margaret

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:53.820
St. James, finishing the job that Scarka started
years earlier in the woods of Ohio.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:58.460
Miles isn't a kid you want in the back seat
of your car, and Sarah knows this.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:01.990
Earlier in the movie, the boy stabbed his
dad with a big pair of garden scissors.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:06.190
That's the kind of thing that would totally
ruin Sarah's plan, so before she starts the

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:10.360
long drive to see Margaret St. James, she
gives Miles a motion sickness pill that'll

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:13.160
put him to sleep and thwart any unexpected
stabbings.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:15.500
"It's big relief for your little traveler.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:17.160
Dramamine for kids!"

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.130
It appears to work.

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:23.760
When they finally arrive at St. James' house,
Miles is still fast asleep, giving Sarah time

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.320
to take care of the business without his interference.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:30.560
Unfortunately, she forgot the foreshadowing
provided by Arthur Jacobson earlier in the

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.200
film: Scarka is really, really good at pretending
to be asleep.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:39.030
He literally said those words out loud just
days before Sarah tried to drug Miles to sleep.

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:40.940
Of course the boy's awake, ya dingus!

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:43.370
He's possessed by an evil fake-sleeping genius!

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:47.210
So when Miles suddenly shows up in the kitchen,
it's because Scarka was in control the whole

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:50.220
time, waiting patiently until the right moment
to strike.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:53.700
And it isn't long before we find out how much
control he actually has.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:58.920
At this point in The Prodigy, two things are
clear: Scarka is more powerful than we'd realized,

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:00.700
and Sarah is kind of a terrible mom.

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:04.400
Put them together, and the plan that's been
unfolding in secret this whole time suddenly

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:05.180
comes to light.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:09.100
Everything that Sarah has been doing to help
her son — kicking her husband out of the

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:13.560
house, driving to Ohio, trying to kill St.
James — has all been carefully orchestrated

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:14.300
by Scarka.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:14.880
Why?

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.020
Well, Scarka really does want to kill Margaret
St. James, like Sarah guessed, but he's stuck

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:20.270
in the body of a minor.

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:22.680
He can't just hop in the car and drive there
himself.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:26.020
To that end, he's been subtly manipulating
Sarah from the start.

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:29.980
Remember when Sarah learns about Edward Scarka
on her laptop at night, with Miles lurking

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:30.960
in the next room?

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.100
Sarah wasn't coming closer to stopping Scarka
with that knowledge, as audiences were led

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:39.000
to believe — Scarka was letting her discover
the awful truth on purpose.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:41.360
Those breadcrumbs run through the entire film.

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:45.900
A baseboard is just so carefully left askew,
leading Sarah to discover a hidden stash of

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:47.610
information on St. James.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:52.720
At her most vulnerable moments, Miles surfaces
as a scared, innocent boy, stringing Sarah

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:55.140
along with the hope that she still has a chance
to fix him.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:07:00.400
In the film's final twist, we realize that
killing St. James wasn't the miracle cure

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:01.560
Sarah had been hoping for.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:05.870
Scarka's still in control of Miles' body,
and he's not planning on going anywhere anytime

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:06.870
soon.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:10.770
After manipulating Sarah into taking him to
St. James, Scarka now has another problem

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:12.330
on his hands: Sarah.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:16.750
With John on life support in the hospital
and Jacobson cowed into silence, Sarah is

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:18.860
the last person who could threaten his new
life.

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:19.780
She's gotta go.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:23.740
Ever manipulative, Scarka baits her with the
truth: Miles is gone.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:24.740
She's too late.

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:28.700
In fact, he claims that Miles hasn't been
part of the equation since at least the halfway

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:29.760
point of the movie.

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:34.420
All of Jacobson's talk about a "war between
souls" really meant nothing, because Scarka

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.700
already destroyed the part of him that was
Miles.

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:40.560
Sarah takes the bait, threatening her son
with the same gun she'd planned to use on

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:41.080
St. James.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:42.620
It went down sort of like this:

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.500
"See you in hell, dinner plate."

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:53.020
Except she goes through with it, because all
that ruckus draws the attention of a farmer,

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:55.240
who shoots Sarah before she can pull the trigger.

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:57.080
Once again, Sarah is too late.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.140
The final moments of the film see young Miles
being dropped off with his new foster family.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:06.560
All innocence and smiles, the boy steps out
of the car to greet his new mom and dad.

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:10.360
In one last scene before the screen cuts to
black, we see Miles in his new bedroom with

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:14.750
old man Scarka looking back at him from his
reflection in the mirror, because ambiguity

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:15.640
is for suckers.

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:19.120
“ahh!”

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:23.140
The message is clear: Scarka is free to kill
again and again.

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.740
Even better, he's not a lumpy old man anymore.

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:29.650
He's a young boy who can manipulate anyone
with his sweetness, making him the ultimate

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:31.020
wolf in sheep's clothing.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:33.840
But while that's the ending we see, is it
really the ending?

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:38.840
At various times throughout the movie, Miles
managed to exert some control over Scarka.

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:42.680
Most notably, he did this when he was being
hypnotized by Arthur Jacobson.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:46.820
Although Scarka seemingly had total control
in that scene, Miles still managed to scratch

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:49.960
a legible message into a leather couch with
just his fingernails.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:54.830
That's not an easy thing to do, and it suggests
that Miles' mind may still be a bigger presence

00:08:54.830 --> 00:08:58.780
than Scarka is willing to admit … maybe
even bigger than Scarka realizes.

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:03.420
After finishing off his final victim, Scarka
tells Sarah in no uncertain terms that her

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:05.060
little boy is gone forever.

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:07.190
Miles hasn't actually been in there for awhile.

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:10.700
He doesn't even try to let her down easy,
after all that she did for him.

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:11.710
Real mature, Scarka.

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:16.500
"I'm sure that we can handle the situation
maturely just like the responsible adults

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:17.000
that we are.

00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:19.860
"Isn’t that right, Mr. Poopy Pants."

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:23.780
But hold on, because lies come in many forms
throughout The Prodigy.

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:28.900
Jacobson also made it clear that Scarka is
a masterful liar and a genius manipulator,

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:31.200
so who's to say where his lies actually end?

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.400
Would he admit to Sarah that part of Miles
is still alive in there somewhere?

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:38.040
Not if he wanted her to wave a gun at him
so that hero farmer would step in.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:40.740
He'd pretend to have full control over the
boy's body.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:41.740
But does he really?

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:45.210
It's entirely possible that Miles is still
in there somewhere, and he's still capable

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:48.000
of coming back and stopping Scarka in the
future.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:51.660
With a movie like this, anything's possible,
whether it makes sense...or not.

